#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209c1be4-3a34-540e-ad62-a9016b6051be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355072} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1a4f0b-5a11-5505-92da-bbed3daccd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355073} {:answer/id #uuid "32048b7d-9771-5112-9667-b14d4fcdbb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355074} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8644ab-88d9-5244-b5f5-3f2f1ec5cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355075} {:answer/id #uuid "12b915b0-a98f-5337-a736-6c6554a559d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f8644ab-88d9-5244-b5f5-3f2f1ec5cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355075} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fab4866-827a-56de-b855-dfa2ae1f942d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdd09f97-d997-5c93-a13a-84264c1c737b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113624" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(z+4x)}^{12}$ is" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(z+4x)}^{12},$ we have $a=z,$ $b=4x,$ and $n=12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+4x)}^{12}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){z}^{12}(4x)^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){z}^{12-1}(4x)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){z}^{12-2}(4x)^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){z}^{12-12}(4x)^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){z}^{12-3}(4x)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){z}^{12-3}(4x)^{3} & =220{z}^{9}\\cdot {4}^{3}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =220{z}^{9}\\cdot 64{x}^{3} \\\\  & =14\\,080{z}^{9}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ is $14\\,080.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6731b8d3-c75f-5333-885e-0feff643626a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355079} {:answer/id #uuid "007019df-ff07-5959-8ca9-140761569f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355080} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5afee8-da55-57fb-a378-1c5578c8579f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355081} {:answer/id #uuid "a36aebbc-507a-5476-9320-7cec4d0441bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<{log}_{6}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355082} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8aec59-c3fc-5506-8b8b-617e897aaebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2-{log}_{6}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5afee8-da55-57fb-a378-1c5578c8579f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c016a94-9cd3-524b-8bd4-be6f5a8e953c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aa91897-5bf6-5f57-bd0a-cbf92ece54f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115227" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4<1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4 & <1 \\\\ 2\\cdot {6}^{x+1} & <-3 \\\\ {6}^{x+1} & <-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $6>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${6}^{x+1}$ cannot be less than $-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}<-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7439a96f-07b1-5673-a103-af2c63c25d18" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Final Velocity of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d943790d-71a5-5114-89a7-107d44792a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{109}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d943790d-71a5-5114-89a7-107d44792a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{109}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355086} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aefd7f2-a708-55bf-82dd-60a253a0f88e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e440856-bf27-5b09-9bf6-1a76f6e12c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-319033" :question/problem "The initial velocity of a particle is $(6i-2j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the **exact** speed of the particle after $3$ hours if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(-i+4j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.$\n\n$|v|=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{km/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=6i-2j,\\,a=-i+4j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=3\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(6i-2j)+(-i+4j)(3) \\\\  & =(6i-2j)+(-3i+12j) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+10\\,j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v| & =|3i+10j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{109}\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc5ff56a-a486-5632-87d0-e3abe3ec42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5ff56a-a486-5632-87d0-e3abe3ec42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355089} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bba9a2f-56ac-5ee8-af64-b2cf3bb29e01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad20e4ae-50bb-514d-b6e0-4e446039e782" :question/math-academy-id "q-319550" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-22}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67f41769-a0ff-5ecc-8b05-5890a7ac9fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d466fa37-4dbd-5bc1-90f5-a483342aca7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arctan }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355092} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d86cc3-669c-5964-882e-1c42ec44a5d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsin }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355093} {:answer/id #uuid "ca60aae5-fefe-55a0-b732-2c02a56f4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355094} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb6a40-a1aa-56fa-8943-666c2856c542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355095} {:answer/id #uuid "75d1b04e-6fd9-5bde-9b44-097f1ca2de5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ arctan }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca60aae5-fefe-55a0-b732-2c02a56f4ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355094} :answer-field/id #uuid "da9baa89-e738-5ea9-88d9-5553bd227a8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42a80aa1-518c-5f74-8db5-8f4e1d84fad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-71775" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}},$ then $f(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\text{ arcsec }x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ea040a-949f-5ee7-a5c0-ecc5e6823ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355099} {:answer/id #uuid "9030266e-a7d6-5513-85b7-ce7af02644c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355100} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb96c1b-a44d-59ba-b4c2-187161e6e063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355101} {:answer/id #uuid "3961da7b-c7be-59c8-ad1d-8d844900f29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355102} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b8ac5a-3d51-557b-b060-964c7f8e2364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9030266e-a7d6-5513-85b7-ce7af02644c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c740d5a0-e7a2-5a44-8007-fac13dcede8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96148112-5e3c-5abe-916c-66cb2646d5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-8574" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=-0.252\\,680…≈-0.252\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-0.252\\,7$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.252\\,7.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.252\\,7.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.252\\,7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066983/assets/8cb3c3ad525f606ec5cc6ef3961577666ab7117b3ce2a17014df989dcb105f57.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.252\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.394\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & =3.394\\,3-2\\pi ≈-2.888\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-0.252\\,7)\\cdot (-2.888\\,9)≈0.7,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place."}]}
